    "summary": "The Somali Federal Government has denied closing Beledweyne airport and called for the removal of militia forces loyal to Ugaas Yusuf from the facility, according to the Minister of Transport and Aviation.",
    "content": "The Somali Federal Government (SFG) has denied reports that it closed Beledweyne Airport, clarifying that the facility remains open for civilian use. The Minister of Transport and Aviation stated that the government has requested the withdrawal of armed forces loyal to Ugaas Yusuf from the airport premises, emphasizing the need to ensure the airport operates without military interference.\n\nThe situation in Beledweyne, the capital of Hiiraan region, has been tense in recent months due to a dispute between the federal government and local clan militias led by Ugaas Yusuf. The Ugaas has reportedly deployed his fighters to strategic locations, including the airport, amid disagreements over security and political arrangements in the region. The federal government's call for the removal of these forces is part of efforts to assert control over key infrastructure.\n\nThe Minister's statement comes after confusion over the airport's status, with some reports suggesting it had been closed. The denial aims to reassure the public and international partners that air travel to Beledweyne is not disrupted. The government insists that the airport should be a neutral zone, free from armed presence, to facilitate humanitarian and commercial flights.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, Beledweyne Airport serves as a vital link to the region, enabling travel and the delivery of remittances and humanitarian aid. Many diaspora members have family in Hiiraan and rely on the airport for access. Any disruption or militarization of the facility could hinder these connections and exacerbate the already fragile situation in the region. The diaspora has been closely following the developments, concerned about the potential impact on local communities.\n\nThe federal government's request for the removal of Ugaas Yusuf's forces underscores the broader challenge of extending state authority over local armed groups. The Ugaas, a traditional elder with significant influence, has yet to respond publicly to the call. The situation remains fluid, and it is unclear whether the militia will comply. The government has urged dialogue to resolve the issue peacefully.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/dfs-oo-dalbatay-in-ciidamada-taabacsan-ugaas-yuusuf-laga-saaro-garoonka-beledweyne/428034/",
